==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|bigot||a sanctimonious person; a religious hypocrite}}, from {{der|en|frm|bigot}}, from {{der|en|fro|bigot}}, of uncertain origin. It is often thought to derive from an Old French derogatory term applied to the religious {{w|Normans}}, said to be known for frequently swearing {{der|en|enm|bi God||by God}} (compare {{cog|ang||bī god}}, {{cog|gmh||bī got}}, {{cog|dum||bi gode}}), which in any case is thought to be the origin of the surname {{m|en|Bigott}}, {{m|en|Bygott}}. (Compare the French use of {{m|frm|goddams|"goddamns"}} to refer to the English in Joan of Arc's time.) Liberman however thinks this has "too strong a taste of a folk etymological guess invented in retrospect" and prefers Grammont et al.'s theory that it derives from {{m|fro|Albigot||[[Albigensian]] heretic}}.<ref>[http://blog.oup.com/2011/10/bigot-2/ OUP blog]</ref> From meaning someone overly (hypocritically or superstitiously) religious it came to mean someone overly devoted to their own religious opinion, and then to its current sense.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪɡət/}}, {{enPR|bĭgʹət}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪɡət}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who is [[narrow-minded]]ly devoted to one's own [[idea]]s and [[group]]s, and [[intolerant]] of (people of) [[differ]]ing ideas, [[race]]s, [[gender]]s, [[religion]]s, [[politics]], etc.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} One who is [[overly]] [[pious]] in matters of [[religion]], often [[hypocritically]] or else [[superstitiously]] so.
#* '''1653''', Urquhart, translating ''Gargantua and Pantagruel'' by François Rabelais, book 1:
#*: He is no '''bigot''' or hypocrite, he is not torn and divided betwixt reality and appearance, no wretch of a rugged and peevish disposition, but honest, jovial, resolute, and a good fellow.
#* '''1664''', Henry More, ''A Modest Enquiry Into the Mystery of Iniquity'', page 436:
#*: Thus one part of their Church becomes Sotts and '''Bigots'''; and the other that behold this Scene of things, though they profess themselves of their Church, become a company of profane Atheists and clancular Deriders of all Religion. {{...}} Nay it is a question whether those that do more superstitiously cleave to them, doe it not rather in a kind of confusion and obstupefaction of mind out of fear and suspicion, then any determinate assurance or firm belief of the things they outwardly profess.
#* '''1820''', {{w|Charles Maturin}}, ''{{w|Melmoth the Wanderer}}'', volume 3:
#*: Donna Clara was a woman of a cold and grave temper, with all the solemnity of a Spaniard, and all the austerity of a '''bigot'''.

